;
; DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0 -- Individual Computers X-Surf 100, Zorro II
;
; Copy this into DEVS:NetInterfaces and call the copy "eth0", then
; either reboot or type
;
;     C:AddNetInterface DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0
;
; The file name is the interface name.  There is no NAME= keyword.
; See the file "eth0" in this drawer for every keyword there is.
;
; The X-Surf 100 is the faster later card and has its own driver.
; xsurf.device does not drive it.
; AmiNetXDuo's anxnet.device also supports it. To use that driver,
; change DEVICE below to anxnet.device and add CARD=xsurf100.
;
; With anxnet.device, enable IEEE 802.3x flow control (PAUSE) on the
; switch port connected to the Amiga. Keep speed/duplex on Auto.
; The driver negotiates PAUSE; no interface flow-control keyword is
; needed. Leave RXBUFFER unset to follow the negotiated allowance.
; Without PAUSE the stack uses a smaller TCP window; a larger RXBUFFER
; cannot prevent the card's small receive ring from overflowing.
; For x-surf-100.device, consult the vendor's flow-control guidance.
; See "X-Surf 100 and flow control" in Docs/AmiNetXDuo.guide.
;
; Optional, with an updated anxnet.device only: uncomment LINKSPEED=10
; to advertise only 10 Mbit/s modes, still negotiating duplex and PAUSE.
; Leave the switch port on Auto. Omit it or use LINKSPEED=AUTO to return
; to normal modes. Reboot or remove/re-add the interface after editing.
; This is a fallback to try on slower CPUs, not a measured speed boost.
; Unsupported cards and older drivers refuse the request.
;LINKSPEED=10
;

DEVICE=x-surf-100.device
UNIT=0

; Ask the network for an address.  If nothing on the network hands
; out addresses, change this to STATIC and fill in ADDRESS/NETMASK
; below.  See "eth0-static" for a worked example.
CONFIGURE=DHCP

;ADDRESS=192.168.1.10
;NETMASK=255.255.255.0
